      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to determine the incremental prognostic value of myocardial contrast echocardiography (MCE) over two-dimensional echocardiography (2DE) in patients undergoing supine bicycle stress."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Eighty-four patients with known or suspected coronary artery disease who underwent supine bicycle stress with 2DE and MCE (mean age, 58.5 +/- 9.7 years; 68 men) were followed up for 48.3 +/- 8.9 months for cardiac death (n = 1), nonfatal myocardial infarction (n = 9), and revascularization (n = 20)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In sequential Cox models, the predictive power of the clinical model was strengthened by 2DE (chi(2) = 7.73 vs 12.92, P = .02) and further improved by MCE (chi(2) = 19.04, P = .01). On multivariate analysis, the only independent follow-up event predictor was ischemia on MCE (hazard ratio, 6.79; 95% confidence interval, 2.02-22.82; P = .001). Among patients with normal results on 2DE, those with normal results on MCE had greater 4.5-year event-free survival than those with abnormal results on MCE (93% vs 69%, P = .01)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "MCE enhances the predictive power of supine bicycle stress 2DE and allows the risk stratification of patients with normal results on 2DE."
        }
      ]},
